2026-07-15 15:05:01,121 | INFO | HTTP Request: POST http://127.0.0.1:11434/api/chat "HTTP/1.1 200 OK"
2026-07-15 15:05:01,121 | AGENT | SecurityCompliance Agent answered: As a Security and Compliance officer at Faber IA, I can confirm that our company has implemented various measures to ensure GDPR compliance, professional secrecy, confidentiality of client data, and respect for auditor independence. 

We have obtained ISO 27001 certification since 2023, which covers the development, delivery, and operations of our services, including data protection. Additionally, we hold ISO 27701 certification, which is an extension for privacy information management.

To ensure GDPR compliance, we have implemented strict data protection measures, including encryption in transit using TLS 1.3 and encryption at rest using AES-256. Our key management infrastructure is based in the EU, ensuring that sensitive data is protected and managed within the region.

We also implement client file partitioning with logical isolation per client and per mission, as well as access controls to prevent any cross-client circulation of data. Pseudonymisation of personal data is also used where relevant, and we apply data minimization principles to ensure that only necessary data is processed.

Our hosting partner, OuraneCloud, provides a SecNumCloud-qualified infrastructure, which supports sovereign hosting of confidential data within the EU. This ensures that our clients' sensitive data is hosted and processed in compliance with GDPR requirements and is not transferred outside the EU.

To support internal quality control and statutory-audit review, we have implemented various security controls, including secure software development life cycle practices such as code review, dependency scanning, secrets management, and periodic penetration testing. Our incident response plan includes detection, notification, and remediation procedures, as well as a 72-hour GDPR breach-notification workflow.

We also maintain audit logs of access and system outputs, which can be used to support statutory-audit reviews. Our data processing register is available, and we have appointed a Data Protection Officer (DPO) to oversee our data protection practices. Our Data Processing Agreement (DPA) is aligned with GDPR Article 28 requirements.

Regarding explainability and auditability, our product, Atelier, provides features such as retrieval trace, audit log, and figure source linking, which support internal quality control and statutory-audit review. We also ensure that our numeric outputs are grounded in sources and can be traced back to the original data.

Overall, we believe that our existing security controls, certifications, and data protection measures demonstrate our commitment to ensuring GDPR compliance, professional secrecy, confidentiality of client data, and respect for auditor independence, as required by the tender.
